// `app:font` — a glyph atlas and the metrics a layout engine needs.
//
//     import "app/font"
//
//     (font.Font f, bool from_disk) = font.load_file(font.default_path(), 16.0)
//     defer font.destroy(&f)
//
//     font.Glyph g = font.glyph(&f, 'A')
//     f32 wide = font.measure(&f, "hello, world")
//
// Three things a text view asks for that a `measure` cannot: where the tab
// stops are, which byte the mouse landed on, and where an underline goes.
//
//     font.Tabs t = font.tabs(4.0 * font.cell_width(&f), 4)
//     f32 caret = font.advance_to(&f, line, cursor_byte, t)
//     (i64 at, i32 col) = font.index_at_x(&f, line, mouse_x, t)
//     (f32 pos, f32 thick, bool real) = font.underline(&f)
//
// **What this package answers is *where the ink is*, and nothing about how it
// reaches a screen.** It rasterises a TrueType face into one RGBA8 image and
// reports, per codepoint, the rectangle of that image the glyph occupies, the
// offset from the pen to that rectangle, and how far the pen then moves. A
// drawing surface turns those into two triangles; this package never sees one,
// which is why it has no dependency on `app:draw` and can be tested with no
// GPU and no window.
//
// ## Every texel is white and the coverage is the alpha
//
// `(255, 255, 255, coverage)`, and the three colour bytes are not padding.
// `sokol_gp`'s stock shader is `texture(tex, uv) * color`: a white texel hands
// the tint's rgb back unchanged and an alpha texel scales the tint's alpha, so
// the product is `vec4(tint.rgb, tint.a * coverage)` — which is exactly what
// `app:draw`'s A8 fragment computed, and it now computes it with no shader at
// all. That fragment was carried as three source strings chosen off
// `sg_query_backend()` — GLSL 4.10, GLSL ES 3.00 and MSL — of which one is
// pixel-verified, one has never been compiled by any machine in this tree, and
// three further backends had no string at all. This format deletes the reason
// every one of them existed.
//
// **It costs four times the memory and that is the whole of the cost.** The
// fallback face at 16 px packs a 256x256 atlas, so 64 kB becomes 256 kB; the
// ceiling — width 128, height `MAX_ATLAS_H` — was 512 kB and is 2 MB. Nothing
// else moves: the boxes, the packer, the metrics and the two counters are what
// they were.
//
// **The unwritten texel is `(255, 255, 255, 0)` and not four zeroes.** A
// transparent *black* texel fringes dark wherever a bilinear sample straddles
// the edge of a box, because what the sample interpolates towards is black; a
// transparent *white* one cannot, because what it interpolates towards is the
// colour the tint already is. That is the single easiest thing to get wrong
// here, so `pack` fills the whole image that way before anything is inked and
// `grow_atlas` fills the rows it adds the same way.
//
// ### Why the rasteriser writes somewhere else first
//
// `stbtt_MakeCodepointBitmap` and `stbtt_MakeGlyphBitmap` write **one coverage
// byte per pixel at a byte stride**. No argument makes them skip three bytes
// and there is no second entry point that writes four, so nothing stb offers
// can write into an RGBA image. A glyph is therefore rasterised into a
// **per-font scratch buffer** — one, allocated once at load, sized to the
// largest box this face can produce at this scale from
// `stbtt_GetFontBoundingBox` — and expanded from there into the atlas.
//
// The alternative considered was a full-size A8 shadow of the atlas, expanded
// wholesale at upload time. That holds `atlas_w * atlas_h` more bytes for the
// life of the `Font`, and it re-expands the whole image where the scratch
// expands only the box that changed. Measured on DejaVu Sans Mono at 32 px: the
// atlas is 256x512, so the shadow would be **128 kB** and the scratch — the
// face's own bounding box at this scale — is 37 by 40, **1480 bytes**. The
// fallback face allocates neither, because its blitter writes texels directly
// and never reaches stb at all.
//
// ## The two sources, behind one type
//
// A `Font` comes from one of two places and a caller cannot tell which without
// asking `source`:
//
//   * **`Source.TrueType`** — `stb_truetype` rasterised the outlines of a real
//     `.ttf` or `.ttc`. Kerning is real, the face may be proportional, and the
//     metrics are the face's own.
//   * **`Source.Fallback`** — the 8x8 monochrome ASCII bitmap compiled into
//     this file as `FALLBACK_BITS`, scaled up by an integer factor. Always
//     available, needs no file, no allocator luck and no font on the machine.
//
// Both fill the same `Font`, so **every function below works on either** and a
// program that only ever draws text does not branch. That is the whole point
// of the fallback: a text editor on a machine with no fonts still shows its
// buffer.
//
// ## Ownership
//
// `load` **copies** the bytes it is handed. `stbtt_fontinfo` holds a pointer
// into the font file and this package keeps it alive for kerning, so a `Font`
// that borrowed the caller's buffer would answer `measure` out of freed memory
// the moment that buffer went away. One copy at load, freed by `destroy`.
//
// ## Where a `Font`'s memory comes from
//
// **No factory here takes an allocator.** `load`, `load_file` and `fallback`
// allocate to the ambient one, so where a face lands is a sentence the caller
// writes once, at the call, and never threads through its own signatures:
//
//     Font f = fallback(16.0)                              // the caller's default
//     Font f = fallback(16.0) using arena.allocator(&a)    // the face lives in `a`
//
// This used to be an `Allocator` parameter on all three, which put the choice
// in the callee's parameter list and made every intermediate function carry a
// value it had no use for. `using` reaches the whole dynamic extent, so one
// word at the outermost call places the copied `.ttf` bytes, the
// `stbtt_fontinfo`, the dense glyph table, the scratch box and the atlas
// image alike.
//
// **`Font.alloc` survives, and it is not there so `destroy` can free.**
// Freeing never needed it — `#delete` uses the allocator the `ref` already
// carries, so every line of `destroy` works with the slot gone. It is there
// because a `Font` keeps allocating *after* it is loaded, and nowhere near the
// load: `add_codepoint` mints the sparse `extra_cp`/`extra` pair on the first
// non-ASCII codepoint anybody measures, and `grow_atlas` remakes `pixels` when
// a shelf runs out. Both happen at a draw, under whatever ambient the drawing
// code is standing in. Reading the ambient once, at load, is what keeps a
// face's later growth in the same place as the face — and a glyph added from
// inside a scratch frame out of an arena that is about to roll back.
//
// ## Coordinates and signs, stated once
//
// Pixels, y **down**, the pen on the baseline — `stb_truetype`'s convention,
// not inverted, because inverting it here would put the one place the two
// disagree inside a package that cannot test the difference.
//
//   * `ascent` is **positive**: pixels from the baseline up to the top of the
//     face.
//   * `descent` is **negative**: pixels from the baseline down to the bottom.
//     That is `stbtt_GetFontVMetrics`' own sign and this package does not
//     "fix" it, because a caller that adds `ascent - descent` and a caller
//     that adds `ascent + descent` must not both look right.
//   * `Glyph.bearing_y` is the offset from the baseline to the **top** of the
//     glyph's box, so it is negative for anything that rises above the
//     baseline — which is nearly everything.
//   * `Glyph.bearing_x` is the offset from the pen to the **left** of the box.
//
// ## Codepoints outside ASCII, and how one gets added
//
// `load` packs **32..126** densely. `add_codepoint` packs anything else, one
// at a time, from the shelf packer's retained cursor — so an atlas grows to fit
// the text a program actually meets rather than to fit Unicode.
//
// **The hard part is not the packing, it is telling the drawing surface.** A
// rasterised glyph is bytes in `pixels`; the GPU holds a copy made at upload
// time, and nothing in this package may know that. The answer is a
// **monotonic generation counter** — `atlas_generation` — bumped by anything
// that writes a pixel. A consumer keeps its own last-seen number and re-uploads
// when the two differ.
//
//     if font.atlas_generation(&f) != g_uploaded:
//         _ = draw.texture_update(g_atlas, font.atlas_pixels(&f))
//         g_uploaded = font.atlas_generation(&f)
//
// The three candidates and why this one:
//
//   * **A callback** puts an arbitrary consumer inside this package's call
//     stack at rasterise time. That is a layering inversion — `app:font`
//     compiles against no drawing surface, which is the claim the package
//     exists to make — and it fires at the worst moment: sokol allows one
//     update per image per frame, and `add_codepoint` is naturally called from
//     the middle of a paint pass.
//   * **A `flush` that reports and clears** is a *one-shot*. Two consumers of
//     one face — a UI backend and a client's own overlay — cannot both learn,
//     because whichever asks first consumes the fact. That is a defect that
//     appears only in the program that has two consumers, which is the worst
//     time to find it.
//   * **A counter** is a value with no owner. It can be read any number of
//     times by any number of consumers, each keeping its own watermark; it
//     costs one `i32`; and each consumer chooses *when* to compare, which is
//     what makes it fit sokol's once-per-frame rule instead of fighting it.
//
// ## The atlas never moves, and grows only when it is asked to
//
// **`add_codepoint` never grows.** When the shelf runs out of room it answers
// `false` and the codepoint keeps answering `.notdef`, exactly as before — a
// grow is a texture the drawing surface has to *remake*, and no consumer
// expects one in the middle of a paint pass. Who asks is the caller, and when
// is between frames:
//
//     if !font.add_codepoint(&f, cp):
//         if font.grow_atlas(&f):
//             _ = font.add_codepoint(&f, cp)
//
// `grow_atlas` doubles the height and keeps the width. Every glyph therefore
// stays on the pixel it was already on and the image is *copied*, not
// re-rasterised — and because a `Glyph` carries a **pixel** rectangle and
// nothing normalised, **no `Glyph` this package has handed out is stale after a
// grow.** A box at `(px, py)` of `pw` by `ph` is at `(px, py)` of `pw` by `ph`,
// and a pixel source rectangle is what `sgp_draw_textured_rect` takes, so
// nothing divides by a denominator that moved.
//
// **That was not always so, and the repair it needed is worth recording rather
// than quietly deleting.** While a `Glyph` carried `u0`/`v0`/`u1`/`v1`, `v` was
// `py / atlas_h`, so a grow staled every glyph ever handed out in `v` alone —
// invisible to a caller, and drawn as the right ink at half the right height. A
// `rewindow` pass walked all 96 dense slots and every sparse one to repair it,
// and the invariant "a `Glyph` is valid for the life of the `Font`" had to be
// narrowed to "until the next `grow_atlas`". Pixel rectangles gave the
// invariant back whole, and `rewindow` went with the hazard it existed for.
//
// **Two things a grow does still cost, and neither is a `Glyph`:**
//
//   * `atlas_pixels` is a **borrow**, and the grow is a `#resize` that may move
//     the block. A `[]u8` taken before a grow points at freed memory after one.
//     Ask again — every consumer already does, because the run it wants is the
//     one it is about to upload.
//   * the texture's **shape** changes, so a consumer owes its drawing surface a
//     destroy and a remake rather than an update. That is `atlas_resizes`, and
//     the next section is the whole argument for why it is a second counter.
//
// ### Why a grow does not bump `atlas_generation`
//
// This is the whole of what keeps a grow from silently corrupting a texture, so
// it is stated here rather than left for a consumer to work out.
//
// `atlas_generation` means *the pixels changed — update the texture in place*.
// An in-place update uploads the texture's **own** byte count; a grown atlas is
// longer than that. `app:draw`'s `texture_update` guards the run it is handed
// with `<` rather than `!=` (`collections/app/draw/device.dado`), so an oversized
// run **passes the guard, is truncated to the old size, and answers `true` — on
// the GPU and on the headless surface alike**. A grow that announced itself
// through `atlas_generation` would hand every consumer already written a silent
// half-upload and a screenful of garbage.
//
// So it does not. **`grow_atlas` leaves `atlas_generation` exactly where it
// was** and bumps `atlas_resizes` instead — a second watermark meaning *the
// atlas has a different shape; destroy the texture and make a new one*. A
// consumer written before growth existed watches `atlas_generation`, never
// calls `grow_atlas`, and cannot reach any of this. A consumer that grows
// watches both, and the resize is tested first:
//
//     if font.atlas_resizes(&f) != g_resized:
//         draw.texture_free(g_atlas)
//         (i32 w, i32 h) = font.atlas_size(&f)
//         g_atlas = draw.texture_create(w, h, font.atlas_pixels(&f))
//         g_resized = font.atlas_resizes(&f)
//         g_uploaded = font.atlas_generation(&f)
//     else if font.atlas_generation(&f) != g_uploaded:
//         _ = draw.texture_update(g_atlas, font.atlas_pixels(&f))
//         g_uploaded = font.atlas_generation(&f)
//
// `draw.texture_create` and not `draw.texture_a8`: there is no A8 texture any
// more, because there is no A8 shader any more. Every texture `app:draw` holds
// is RGBA8 and this atlas is handed over as the RGBA8 run it is.
//
// ### The ceiling
//
// The height doubles and stops at `MAX_ATLAS_H`. Past it `grow_atlas` answers
// `false` and changes nothing at all — `add_codepoint` keeps answering `false`
// and `glyph` keeps answering `.notdef`, which is what a program had before
// growth existed, some doublings later. There is no unbounded case.
package font

import "core:mem"
import "core:io"
import "core:os/fs"
import "core:chars"
import "core:strings"
import "vendor:stb/stb_truetype"

// ── the surface ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Where a `Font`'s pixels came from. A caller that must know — to warn, to
// pick a different size, to disable ligatures it will never get — asks; a
// caller that only draws does not.
enum Source: (Fallback, TrueType)

// The first and last codepoints `load` packs densely. Everything between them
// is present; `glyph` answers `present == false` for a codepoint the face does
// not have, which is not the same as one outside this block.
const char32 FIRST = ' '
const char32 LAST = '~'
private const i32 DENSE = 95      // LAST - FIRST + 1

// The dense run has one slot past the ASCII block, and it holds `.notdef`. It
// is packed, rasterised and measured exactly like a glyph, because it *is* one:
// the whole point of `.notdef` is that a client which draws what it is handed
// is correct without knowing there was anything to handle.
private const i32 NOTDEF = 95
private const i32 SLOTS = 96

// One padding pixel between packed boxes and around the edge, so a bilinear
// sample at a glyph's border cannot reach its neighbour's ink.
private const i32 PAD = 1

// Bytes per texel. Named rather than written as a 4 wherever an index is
// computed, because every one of those is the same fact and a file that spells
// it several ways is a file where one of them can be changed alone.
private const i64 BPP = 4

// The reserved fully-opaque block. It sits at the atlas origin, before the
// packer's first shelf, so no glyph can ever be placed against it.
//
// **What stops a quad drawn over it from fringing is `solid_src`'s zero extent,
// not this size** — and that is worth stating because the plausible belief is
// the other way round. Measured on GLCORE under software Mesa, over a 64×32
// quad, counting pixels that are not exactly the tint:
//
//     source rect   block   wrong pixels
//     a point       1×1     0 of 2048
//     a point       6×6     0 of 2048
//     the block     1×1     2048 of 2048   (corners read 67 where 255 belongs)
//     the block     6×6     644 of 2048
//
// The measurement was made with uv windows and it carries over unchanged,
// because `sgp_draw_textured_rect` divides a pixel source rect by the image
// size and hands the sampler the same two numbers. A source rect with *any*
// extent interpolates across the quad and reaches the padding at its corners
// however large the block is; a rect of zero extent cannot, whatever the quad's
// size, because every fragment resolves to the same texel. So the degeneracy is
// the guard. The block is margin around it — six squared means a sample would
// have to drift three whole texels before it found anything but an opaque white
// — and it costs 144 bytes of RGBA8.
private const i32 SOLID = 6

// A codepoint's place in the atlas, and how to set it down.
//
// `px,py` is the top-left corner **in atlas pixels** and `pw,ph` the extent,
// which is the source rectangle `sgp_draw_textured_rect` takes. There is no
// normalised form of it here and there was one until this package stopped
// needing one: four `f32`s divided by the atlas size, which cost a division per
// glyph at pack time, staled on every `grow_atlas`, and needed a repair pass to
// unstale. A pixel rectangle is invariant under a grow and is what the one
// consumer wanted all along.
//
// A blank glyph — a space, or anything the face draws no ink for — has
// `pw == 0 && ph == 0` and a **nonzero `advance`**. That pair is the whole
// definition of blank and there is no separate flag for it.
//
// `present` is about the *codepoint*, not about the box: a codepoint this atlas
// does not have answers the `.notdef` glyph, which has a real box and a real
// advance and `present == false`. So *blank* and *missing* are two different
// things and neither is inferable from the other.
type Glyph: (
    i32 px, i32 py,
    i32 pw, i32 ph,
    f32 bearing_x, f32 bearing_y,
    f32 advance,
    bool present,
)

// A rasterised face: one RGBA8 image, the boxes cut out of it, and the
// vertical metrics that space one line from the next.
type Font: (
    // Where this face's memory comes from: the ambient as it stood when the
    // face was loaded. Kept for the growth that happens later — `add_codepoint`
    // and `grow_atlas` — and read by nothing that frees. See "Where a `Font`'s
    // memory comes from" in the package doc comment.
    (rawptr(rawptr, AllocatorMode, u64, u64, rawptr, u64) proc, rawptr data) alloc,
    Source source,

    // `atlas_w * atlas_h * BPP` bytes, every texel `(255, 255, 255, coverage)`.
    ref []u8 pixels,
    i32 atlas_w, i32 atlas_h,

    // Where stb rasterises, one glyph at a time, before `expand` widens it into
    // `pixels`. One box, sized at load to the largest this face can produce;
    // nil on a fallback face, which inks texels directly and never calls stb.
    // See the package comment for why this exists at all.
    ref []u8 scratch,

    ref []Glyph dense,
    ref []char32 extra_cp,
    ref []Glyph extra,
    i32 extra_used,

    // The shelf packer's cursor, retained. See the package comment.
    i32 shelf_x, i32 shelf_y, i32 shelf_h,

    // Bumped by anything that writes a pixel. See the package comment: this is
    // the whole of how a drawing surface learns the atlas changed.
    i32 generation,

    // Bumped only by `grow_atlas`, and deliberately not by anything that writes
    // a pixel. See the package comment: `generation` means *update the texture*
    // and this one means *remake it*, and conflating them is a truncated upload
    // that reports success.
    i32 resizes,

    f32 pixel_height,
    f32 ascent_px, f32 descent_px, f32 line_gap_px,
    f32 scale,
    bool monospace,

    // The metrics a text view asks for that the vertical trio cannot answer.
    // `cell_px` is the nominal advance of one character cell; `cap_px` and
    // `xh_px` are measured off this atlas's own ink; the underline pair is read
    // out of the face's `post` table, and `ul_real` says whether it was there.
    f32 cell_px, f32 cap_px, f32 xh_px,
    f32 ul_pos_px, f32 ul_thick_px, bool ul_real,

    // The TrueType source, kept alive because `stbtt_fontinfo` points into it
    // and `measure` asks it for kerning. Both are nil for a fallback font.
    // `ttf_at` is where this face begins inside those bytes — 0 for a plain
    // `.ttf`, and the entry `stbtt_GetFontOffsetForIndex` found for a `.ttc`.
    ref []u8 ttf,
    i32 ttf_at,
    ref stb_truetype.stbtt_fontinfo info,
)

// ── loading ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The tallest face this package makes, in pixels: 16384, taller than any
// display. **Every pixel height is held to `1 ..= MAX_PIXEL_HEIGHT` on the way
// in**, NaN reading as 1, because everything below converts it to an integer —
// a bitmap scale, a shelf reserve, a scratch side, a `.notdef` box — and does
// arithmetic on the result. A float becoming an integer saturates, so an
// absurd height used to reach `8 * i32(2^31 - 1)` in `fallback`, which is an
// `i32` overflow and a trap, and a NaN one passed the old `< 1.0` guard. With
// the hold, each of those conversions is of a value provably in range.
private const f32 MAX_PIXEL_HEIGHT = 16384.0

private f32 held_pixel_height(f32 pixel_height):
    if !(pixel_height >= 1.0):
        return 1.0
    if pixel_height > MAX_PIXEL_HEIGHT:
        return MAX_PIXEL_HEIGHT
    return pixel_height

// `floor`, in `f32` and without `core:math`'s `f64` round trip, for the tab
// stop in `cell_at`. It converts only a value whose integer part an `i32`
// holds: at 2^23 and past it every `f32` is already a whole number, so it is
// its own floor, and NaN is NaN.
private f32 floor_f(f32 x):
    if !(x > -8388608.0 && x < 8388608.0):
        return x
    f32 t = f32(i32(x))
    if t > x:
        t = t - 1.0
    return t

// Rasterise `ttf_bytes` at `pixel_height` and answer the atlas.
//
// **Never fails into an unusable value**: bytes that are not a font, a font
// with no glyphs, or an allocator that cannot supply the atlas all answer the
// built-in fallback at the same pixel height, and `source` says so. There is
// no half-built `Font` and nothing here returns nil.
//
// `.ttc` collections work: the offset of face 0 comes from
// `stbtt_GetFontOffsetForIndex`, which reads the `ttcf` tag, and a plain
// `.ttf` answers 0 from the same call. So one path serves both and there is no
// extension sniffing anywhere in this package.
//
// It takes no allocator: everything it mints comes from the ambient one, so
// `load(bytes, 16.0) using a` puts the whole face in `a`. See "Where a
// `Font`'s memory comes from" in the package doc comment.
Font load([]u8 ttf_bytes, f32 pixel_height):
    f32 ph = held_pixel_height(pixel_height)

    i64 n = #len(ttf_bytes)
    if n <= 0:
        return fallback(ph)

    ref []u8 own = #make([]u8, n)
    if own == nil:
        return fallback(ph)
    i64 zero = 0
    for i in zero..<n:
        own[i] = ttf_bytes[i]

    ref stb_truetype.stbtt_fontinfo info = #new(stb_truetype.stbtt_fontinfo)
    if info == nil:
        #delete(own)
        return fallback(ph)

    i32 offset = stb_truetype.stbtt_GetFontOffsetForIndex(&own[0], 0)
    if offset < 0:
        #delete(info)
        #delete(own)
        return fallback(ph)

    if stb_truetype.stbtt_InitFont(info, &own[0], offset) == 0:
        #delete(info)
        #delete(own)
        return fallback(ph)

    Font f
    f.alloc = #default
    f.source = Source.TrueType
    f.pixel_height = ph
    f.ttf = own
    f.ttf_at = offset
    f.info = info
    f.scale = stb_truetype.stbtt_ScaleForPixelHeight(info, ph)

    i32 asc = 0
    i32 desc = 0
    i32 gap = 0
    stb_truetype.stbtt_GetFontVMetrics(info, &asc, &desc, &gap)
    f.ascent_px = f32(asc) * f.scale
    f.descent_px = f32(desc) * f.scale
    f.line_gap_px = f32(gap) * f.scale

    f.dense = #make([]Glyph, i64(SLOTS))
    if f.dense == nil:
        #delete(info)
        #delete(own)
        return fallback(ph)

    // Where every glyph of this face is rasterised before it is widened into
    // the atlas. stb writes one coverage byte per pixel and the atlas holds
    // four, so there is no way to rasterise straight into it. See the package
    // comment.
    f.scratch = make_scratch(&f)
    if f.scratch == nil:
        #delete(f.dense)
        #delete(info)
        #delete(own)
        return fallback(ph)

    // Pass one: the boxes and the horizontal metrics, no pixels yet.
    for idx in 0..<DENSE:
        i32 cp = i32(FIRST) + idx
        i32 gi = stb_truetype.stbtt_FindGlyphIndex(info, cp)
        ^Glyph g = &f.dense[i64(idx)]
        glyph_metrics(&f, gi, g)
        g.present = gi != 0

    f.cell_px = nominal_cell(&f)

    // `.notdef` is glyph index 0 by definition of the format, so a face that
    // has one is asked for it rather than being second-guessed. A face whose
    // glyph 0 is empty — and there are such faces — gets a box drawn here, in
    // `ink_notdef`, and the box needs a placement, which is why its extent is
    // decided before the pack rather than after it.
    ^Glyph nd = &f.dense[i64(NOTDEF)]
    glyph_metrics(&f, 0, nd)
    nd.present = false
    if nd.pw <= 0 || nd.ph <= 0:
        synth_notdef(&f, nd)
    if nd.advance <= 0.0:
        nd.advance = f.cell_px

    // Pass two: choose an atlas, reserve the solid block, place every box on a
    // shelf.
    if !pack(&f):
        #delete(f.dense)
        #delete(info)
        #delete(own)
        return fallback(ph)

    // Pass three: ink, each glyph through the scratch and out into the atlas.
    for idx in 0..<DENSE:
        ^Glyph g = &f.dense[i64(idx)]
        if g.pw <= 0 || g.ph <= 0:
            continue
        i32 cp = i32(FIRST) + idx
        ink_codepoint(&f, g, cp)
    ink_notdef(&f, nd, 0)

    f.monospace = all_advances_equal(&f)
    measure_heights(&f)
    read_underline(&f)
    return f

// The same, reading the file itself.
//
// **Answers `(Font, bool)` and the `Font` is always usable.** `ok` is false
// when `path` could not be read or was not a font, and the `Font` is then the
// fallback — so a caller that ignores the flag still draws text, and a caller
// that wants to complain has something true to complain about. Returning only
// a `Font` would have hidden a missing file behind a font that looks
// deliberate; returning only a `bool` and a nil `Font` would have made every
// call site branch before it could draw. This is the pair that makes both
// callers honest, and `source` says the same thing a second way for a `Font`
// that has been passed on.
//
// No allocator, for `load`'s reason: the ambient places the face, and the two
// buffers this borrows on the way — the C path string and the file's bytes —
// go to the same place and are given back before it returns.
(Font f, bool ok) load_file(string8 path, f32 pixel_height):
    f32 ph = held_pixel_height(pixel_height)

    if path == "":
        return (fallback(ph), false)

    // `core:strings` still spells its allocator out, so the ambient is handed
    // over by name here. `#default` *is* the ambient, so this is the same
    // placement every other line in this function gets, written where a
    // signature that is not this package's insists on one.
    ref []char8 cpath = strings.clone_to_cstring(path, #default)
    if cpath == nil:
        return (fallback(ph), false)

    (ref []u8 bytes, bool read_ok) = read_all(cstring(cpath))
    #delete(cpath)
    if !read_ok:
        return (fallback(ph), false)

    Font f = load(bytes[..<#len(bytes)], ph)
    #delete(bytes)
    if f.source != Source.TrueType:
        return (f, false)
    return (f, true)

// The built-in 8x8 bitmap, at the nearest whole multiple of 8 that does not
// exceed `pixel_height` (and never smaller than 8, nor larger than 16384 — the
// hold every entry point applies). It is exposed rather than private because
// a program that *wants* the pixel font — a debug overlay, a deliberate look —
// should not have to fail a file read to get one.
//
// No allocator, for `load`'s reason — and `load` calls this on every path it
// gives up on, so the two agree about where a face lands without either of
// them naming a place.
Font fallback(f32 pixel_height):
    i32 s = i32(held_pixel_height(pixel_height) / 8.0)
    if s < 1:
        s = 1

    Font f
    f.alloc = #default
    f.source = Source.Fallback
    i32 cell = 8 * s
    f.pixel_height = f32(cell)
    f.scale = f32(s)
    f.monospace = true
    // The bitmap's baseline sits under row 6: rows 6 and 7 are where every
    // descender in the face lives ('g', 'p', 'q', 'y', ','), and nothing rises
    // above row 0. So six rows up and two rows down, exactly.
    i32 up = 6 * s
    i32 down = 2 * s
    f.ascent_px = f32(up)
    f.descent_px = -f32(down)
    f.line_gap_px = 0.0

    f.dense = #make([]Glyph, i64(SLOTS))
    if f.dense == nil:
        return f

    for idx in 0..<DENSE:
        u64 bits = FALLBACK_BITS[i64(idx)]
        (i32 c0, i32 r0, i32 c1, i32 r1, bool inked) = ink_box(bits)
        ^Glyph g = &f.dense[i64(idx)]
        g.present = true
        g.advance = f32(cell)
        if !inked:
            g.pw = 0
            g.ph = 0
            g.bearing_x = 0.0
            g.bearing_y = 0.0
            continue
        g.pw = (c1 - c0 + 1) * s
        g.ph = (r1 - r0 + 1) * s
        i32 bx = c0 * s
        i32 by = (r0 - 6) * s
        g.bearing_x = f32(bx)
        g.bearing_y = f32(by)

    f.cell_px = f32(cell)

    // The bitmap has 95 glyphs and no 96th, so its `.notdef` is drawn rather
    // than found. Nothing is lost: a bitmap face has no `.notdef` to be
    // faithful to.
    ^Glyph nd = &f.dense[i64(NOTDEF)]
    synth_notdef(&f, nd)
    nd.present = false
    nd.advance = f32(cell)

    if !pack(&f):
        #delete(f.dense)
        f.dense = nil
        return f

    for idx in 0..<DENSE:
        ^Glyph g = &f.dense[i64(idx)]
        if g.pw <= 0 || g.ph <= 0:
            continue
        u64 bits = FALLBACK_BITS[i64(idx)]
        (i32 c0, i32 r0, i32 _, i32 _, bool inked) = ink_box(bits)
        if !inked:
            continue
        blit(&f, g, bits, c0, r0, s)
    ink_notdef(&f, nd, s)

    measure_heights(&f)
    read_underline(&f)
    return f

// Give back everything a `Font` owns. Safe on a fallback (there is no `.ttf`
// and no `stbtt_fontinfo` to release) and safe twice, because every field it
// frees it also nils.
void destroy(^Font f):
    if f == nil:
        return
    if f.pixels != nil:
        #delete(f.pixels)
        f.pixels = nil
    if f.scratch != nil:
        #delete(f.scratch)
        f.scratch = nil
    if f.dense != nil:
        #delete(f.dense)
        f.dense = nil
    if f.extra != nil:
        #delete(f.extra)
        f.extra = nil
    if f.extra_cp != nil:
        #delete(f.extra_cp)
        f.extra_cp = nil
    if f.info != nil:
        #delete(f.info)
        f.info = nil
    if f.ttf != nil:
        #delete(f.ttf)
        f.ttf = nil
    f.atlas_w = 0
    f.atlas_h = 0
    f.extra_used = 0

// ── asking about glyphs ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Where `cp` lives.
//
// **A codepoint this atlas does not have answers `.notdef`** — a real box with
// a real advance, `present == false`. That is what makes the naive client
// correct: a program that draws what it is handed and adds the advance it is
// given puts a visible box on the screen and keeps its columns honest, instead
// of drawing nothing and advancing nothing, which collapses a run of unknown
// text to zero width and makes every caret column after it a lie.
//
// A client that wants to do something smarter still can: `present` is honest,
// and `notdef` names the same glyph so a caller can compare.
Glyph glyph(^Font f, char32 cp):
    Glyph none
    if f == nil || f.dense == nil:
        return none
    if cp >= FIRST && cp <= LAST:
        Glyph g = f.dense[i64(cp) - i64(FIRST)]
        if g.present:
            return g
        return f.dense[i64(NOTDEF)]
    for i in 0..<f.extra_used:
        if f.extra_cp[i64(i)] == cp:
            return f.extra[i64(i)]
    return f.dense[i64(NOTDEF)]

// The glyph every unknown codepoint answers. `present` is false and everything
// else is real.
Glyph notdef(^Font f):
    Glyph none
    if f == nil || f.dense == nil:
        return none
    return f.dense[i64(NOTDEF)]

// Whether this atlas has `cp` itself, as opposed to answering `.notdef` for it.
bool has(^Font f, char32 cp):
    return glyph(f, cp).present

// The advance width of `s`, with kerning where the face has any.
//
// **Characters, not bytes.** The walk decodes UTF-8, so a multi-byte sequence
// measures as one character — the `.notdef` box's advance while the atlas has
// not packed it, and the glyph's own once `add_codepoint` has. Walking bytes
// was the older behaviour and it is why a text view could not use this
// function: a three-byte sequence measured as three unknown bytes, which is
// three boxes wide on screen and one character wide to a caret.
f32 measure(^Font f, string8 s):
    return measure_tabbed(f, s, no_tabs())

// ── measuring the way a text view measures ────────────────────────────────
//
// **Every x position in a text view is the same walk**: where to put a glyph,
// where the caret is, how wide a selection quad is, which character the mouse
// hit, how far to scroll horizontally. `measure` answers one of those and a
// client that needs the other four writes the walk itself — which is what
// happened, five times, in one editor, because `measure` has nowhere to put a
// tab stop and no way to ask about a byte offset.
//
// So the walk is here, once, as a primitive and three folds over it.
//
//     font.Tabs t = font.tabs(4.0 * font.cell_width(&f), 4)
//     f32 caret  = font.advance_to(&f, line, cursor_byte, t)
//     i32 column = font.column_to(&f, line, cursor_byte, t)
//     (i64 at, i32 col) = font.index_at_x(&f, line, mouse_x, t)
//
// A tab advances to the next multiple of `Tabs.width` **measured from the start
// of the run**, which is the only definition that works on a proportional face;
// on a monospace face with `width == n * cell_width` it is exactly the familiar
// every-n-columns stop. `Tabs.columns` is the same stop counted in characters,
// which is what a status line's *Col* is and what nothing in pixels can answer.

// Where the tab stops are: `width` in pixels and `columns` in characters. Both
// zero — `no_tabs()` — makes a tab an ordinary character, drawn as `.notdef`
// like any other codepoint the atlas has no glyph for.
type Tabs: (f32 width, i32 columns)

// The tab stops a text view usually wants: `width` pixels and `columns`
// characters apart.
Tabs tabs(f32 width, i32 columns):
    return (width, columns)

// No tab stops at all.
Tabs no_tabs():
    Tabs t
    return t

// One character of a run, and everything a walk needs to take a step.
//
// `nbytes` is how many bytes the character took, so a walk is `at += nbytes`
// and a size of zero means the end. `columns` is how many character columns it
// used — one for anything but a tab. `tab` says which it was, because a client
// that draws a run must not put `.notdef` where it advanced to a stop.
type Cell: (char32 cp, i32 nbytes, f32 advance, i32 columns, bool tab)

// The character of `s` beginning at byte `at`, as it lands when `pen` pixels
// and `column` characters of the run are already behind it.
//
// **`pen` and `column` are relative to the run's origin**, not to the screen: a
// tab stop is defined against the start of the line and nothing else.
//
// Kerning is *not* in `advance`, because kerning is a property of a pair and
// this answers about one character. The folds below apply it; a client writing
// its own loop calls `kern` between steps, which is what `app:text` does.
Cell cell_at(^Font f, string8 s, i64 at, f32 pen, i32 column, Tabs t):
    Cell none
    i64 n = #len(s)
    if f == nil || at < 0 || at >= n:
        return none
    (char32 cp, i32 taken) = chars.decode(s, i32(at))
    if taken <= 0:
        return none
    if cp == '\t' && t.width > 0.0:
        f32 next = (floor_f(pen / t.width) + 1.0) * t.width
        i32 cols = 1
        if t.columns > 0:
            cols = t.columns - (column % t.columns)
        return (cp, taken, next - pen, cols, true)
    return (cp, taken, glyph(f, cp).advance, 1, false)

// The pen x of byte offset `upto`, measured from the run's origin. An offset
// past the end answers the whole run's width, which is what a caret at the end
// of a line wants.
f32 advance_to(^Font f, string8 s, i64 upto, Tabs t):
    if f == nil:
        return 0.0
    f32 pen = 0.0
    i32 col = 0
    i64 i = 0
    i64 n = #len(s)
    for i < n && i < upto:
        Cell c = cell_at(f, s, i, pen, col, t)
        if c.nbytes <= 0:
            break
        pen = pen + c.advance
        col = col + c.columns
        i64 next = i + i64(c.nbytes)
        if next < n && next <= upto && !c.tab:
            (char32 nc, i32 _) = chars.decode(s, i32(next))
            pen = pen + kern(f, c.cp, nc)
        i = next
    return pen

// The character column of byte offset `upto` — what a status line calls *Col*.
i32 column_to(^Font f, string8 s, i64 upto, Tabs t):
    if f == nil:
        return 0
    f32 pen = 0.0
    i32 col = 0
    i64 i = 0
    i64 n = #len(s)
    for i < n && i < upto:
        Cell c = cell_at(f, s, i, pen, col, t)
        if c.nbytes <= 0:
            break
        pen = pen + c.advance
        col = col + c.columns
        i = i + i64(c.nbytes)
    return col

// The character `want` pixels along the run lands on, rounded to the nearer
// boundary — a caret and a mouse click are the same question and this is it.
// Answers the byte offset and the column, because a client that hit-tests
// almost always wants to report the column too.
(i64 at, i32 column) index_at_x(^Font f, string8 s, f32 want, Tabs t):
    if f == nil:
        return (0, 0)
    f32 pen = 0.0
    i32 col = 0
    i64 i = 0
    i64 n = #len(s)
    for i < n:
        Cell c = cell_at(f, s, i, pen, col, t)
        if c.nbytes <= 0:
            break
        if want < pen + c.advance * 0.5:
            return (i, col)
        pen = pen + c.advance
        col = col + c.columns
        i = i + i64(c.nbytes)
    return (i, col)

// The advance width of the whole run with tab stops applied. `measure` is this
// with no tab stops.
f32 measure_tabbed(^Font f, string8 s, Tabs t):
    return advance_to(f, s, #len(s) + 1, t)

// The kerning adjustment between two codepoints, in pixels. Zero for a
// fallback font, which has no kerning table and would be lying to invent one.
f32 kern(^Font f, char32 a, char32 b):
    if f == nil || f.info == nil:
        return 0.0
    i32 k = stb_truetype.stbtt_GetCodepointKernAdvance(f.info, i32(a), i32(b))
    return f32(k) * f.scale

// ── vertical metrics ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Baseline to baseline: `ascent - descent + line_gap`, with `descent`
// negative, which is why this is a subtraction.
f32 line_height(^Font f):
    if f == nil:
        return 0.0
    return f.ascent_px - f.descent_px + f.line_gap_px

// Pixels above the baseline. Positive.
f32 ascent(^Font f):
    if f == nil:
        return 0.0
    return f.ascent_px

// Pixels below the baseline. **Negative** — see the package comment.
f32 descent(^Font f):
    if f == nil:
        return 0.0
    return f.descent_px

// The size this face was rasterised at.
f32 pixel_height(^Font f):
    if f == nil:
        return 0.0
    return f.pixel_height

// Whether every glyph in the dense block advances the same distance. A layout
// engine that can take a fast path for a fixed grid asks this rather than
// guessing from the file name.
bool monospace(^Font f):
    if f == nil:
        return false
    return f.monospace

// The nominal width of one character cell: the advance of `'0'`, or of a space
// where the face draws no digit.
//
// **On a monospace face this is *the* cell** and every glyph advances by it —
// which is the number a client was taking as `glyph(' ').advance`. On a
// proportional face it is a nominal cell and nothing more: it is what a tab
// stop and a `.notdef` box are sized against, and it is not the width of
// anything you draw.
f32 cell_width(^Font f):
    if f == nil:
        return 0.0
    return f.cell_px

// Pixels from the baseline to the top of a capital letter, measured off this
// atlas's `H`. Positive. Zero for a face that draws no `H`.
f32 cap_height(^Font f):
    if f == nil:
        return 0.0
    return f.cap_px

// Pixels from the baseline to the top of a lowercase `x`, measured off this
// atlas's `x`. Positive. Zero for a face that draws no `x`.
f32 x_height(^Font f):
    if f == nil:
        return 0.0
    return f.xh_px

// Where to draw an underline, and how thick.
//
// `position` is pixels **below the baseline, positive down** — this package's
// own y-down convention, not the face's y-up one — so an underline under a run
// whose baseline is at `b` is a fill at `b + position` of `thickness` high.
//
// `from_face` is false when the pair was derived rather than read out of the
// face's `post` table: the built-in bitmap has no tables, and a real face may
// have no `post`. A derived pair is a reasonable guess and it draws fine; the
// flag is there so a program that cares can say so rather than a program that
// does not care having to.
(f32 position, f32 thickness, bool from_face) underline(^Font f):
    if f == nil:
        return (0.0, 0.0, false)
    return (f.ul_pos_px, f.ul_thick_px, f.ul_real)

// ── the atlas ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The image: RGBA8, row-major, four bytes per texel, stride `atlas_w * 4`.
// Every texel is `(255, 255, 255, coverage)` — see the package comment for why
// the three colour bytes are not padding and why an untouched texel is white
// rather than black.
//
// **A borrow, and `grow_atlas` ends it as surely as `destroy` does.** The grow
// is a `realloc`, which may move the image, so a `[]u8` taken from here before
// a grow points at freed memory afterwards. Ask again after one; every consumer
// already does, because the run it wants is the one it is about to upload.
[]u8 atlas_pixels(^Font f):
    []u8 nothing
    if f == nil || f.pixels == nil:
        return nothing
    return f.pixels[..<i64(f.atlas_w) * i64(f.atlas_h) * BPP]

(i32 w, i32 h) atlas_size(^Font f):
    if f == nil:
        return (0, 0)
    return (f.atlas_w, f.atlas_h)

// How many times this atlas's pixels have changed. Zero for a `Font` that
// never packed. The atlas never moves, so a consumer that uploaded at
// generation `g` and reads a different number needs `texture_update` and
// nothing more — **as long as it also watches `atlas_resizes`**, which is the
// one thing that can make the atlas a different size.
//
// **`grow_atlas` deliberately does not touch this counter.** That is what makes
// a consumer which watches only this one unable to issue an update its texture
// is too small to hold, which `app:draw` would accept and truncate. See the
// package comment for the whole of that argument, and for why this is a counter
// and not a callback or a flush.
i32 atlas_generation(^Font f):
    if f == nil:
        return 0
    return f.generation

// How many times this atlas has changed **shape**. Zero for a `Font` that has
// never grown, which is every `Font` until something calls `grow_atlas`.
//
// A consumer that uploaded at shape `r` and reads a different number owes its
// drawing surface a **destroy and a remake**, not an update: `atlas_pixels` is
// now longer than the texture holds, and `app:draw`'s `texture_update` accepts
// an over-long run, truncates it and answers `true`. The two counters are two
// different instructions and that is the entire reason there are two.
i32 atlas_resizes(^Font f):
    if f == nil:
        return 0
    return f.resizes

// The tallest atlas this package will grow to. **This is the bound, and there
// is one.**
//
// 4096 because it is already the packer's ceiling on the *width* ladder, so the
// two dimensions share one number rather than each carrying its own; and
// because a drawing surface that cannot hold a texture 4096 tall could not have
// held the widest atlas `pack` already produces without growing at all. Past it
// `grow_atlas` answers `false` and nothing moves.
//
// At the width a monospace UI actually gets — 128 — the ceiling is
// 128 x 4096 x 4 = **2 MB** of RGBA8, where the same atlas was 512 kB while
// this package held one coverage byte per pixel. The ceiling counted in
// *codepoints* is
// not a constant and cannot be one: it is however many boxes the shelf walk
// fits, which depends on the face and the pixel height. Measure it; do not
// quote it.
private const i32 MAX_ATLAS_H = 4096

// Double the atlas's height, keeping its width. Answers whether it grew.
//
// **Nothing here grows by itself** — `add_codepoint` answers `false` and stops.
// This is the verb a caller reaches for when it would rather have the codepoint
// than the refusal, and calling it is a decision about a frame boundary rather
// than about a glyph. See the package comment for the loop and for when it is
// safe.
//
// The width does not change, so no box moves, nothing is re-rasterised and
// **every `Glyph` already handed out stays correct**: a `Glyph` carries a pixel
// rectangle and a pixel rectangle is invariant under a grow. This verb used to
// spend that invariant — `v0`/`v1` divided by `atlas_h` — and a `rewindow` pass
// walked every glyph to repair them. Both are gone; the package comment records
// what they were for.
//
// What the grow does still spend is the **borrow**: the image is `#resize`d and
// the new rows filled, so a `[]u8` taken from `atlas_pixels` before this call is
// dangling after it and a caller that kept one asks again.
//
// **The new rows are `(255, 255, 255, 0)`, not four zeroes.** Four zeroes is a
// transparent *black*, and a sample that straddles the boundary between a
// glyph's edge and a black-transparent neighbour interpolates towards black and
// fringes dark. Filling white costs the same stores and cannot.
//
// `atlas_generation` does **not** move. `atlas_resizes` does. A consumer that
// confuses the two gets a truncated upload and a `true` from its drawing
// surface; that is the whole reason for the second counter and it is argued in
// the package comment.
//
// Answers `false`, having changed nothing, when the atlas is already at
// `MAX_ATLAS_H`, when there is no atlas to grow, or when the allocator refused
// the larger image. A `false` is never a broken `Font`: every caller may retry
// `add_codepoint` and get the same `.notdef` it had.
bool grow_atlas(^Font f):
    if f == nil || f.pixels == nil || f.dense == nil:
        return false
    if f.atlas_w < 1 || f.atlas_h < 1:
        return false
    if f.atlas_h > MAX_ATLAS_H / 2:
        return false
    i32 nh = f.atlas_h * 2

    i64 old_n = i64(f.atlas_w) * i64(f.atlas_h)
    i64 new_n = i64(f.atlas_w) * i64(nh)
    if !#resize(f.pixels, new_n * BPP):
        return false
    clear_texels(f, old_n, new_n)

    // Only past the point of no failure is the size published, so a refused
    // `#resize` leaves a `Font` whose every field still describes the atlas it
    // still has.
    f.atlas_h = nh
    f.resizes = f.resizes + 1
    return true

// ── the solid texel ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// A fully-opaque point inside the atlas, as a **zero-extent source rectangle**
// in atlas pixels, ready to hand to a textured-quad call.
//
// **This is what makes a rule, a caret, an underline, a selection quad or a
// replacement box cost nothing.** `app:draw` breaks a batch on a texture
// change, so a run of glyphs with a box drawn between two of them is three
// draw calls, and a screen of them is one call per box — measured, on a real
// client: a file that needed replacement boxes went from 3 draw calls to 20,
// and came back to 9 only by splitting the line painter into an atlas pass and
// a white pass over the same lines. Every mixed-content renderer had to be
// written that way and nothing said so. With this, a rectangle drawn over the
// atlas rides in the glyph batch and the whole class of problem is gone.
//
// **`w` and `h` are zero, and that is the whole mechanism rather than a
// tidiness.** A source rectangle with any extent at all interpolates across the
// quad and reaches the block's transparent padding at its corners, which is a
// visible fringe — measured, every pixel of a 64×32 quad wrong and its corners
// reading 67 where 255 belongs, and 644 of 2048 still wrong with the block six
// texels square. A rectangle of zero extent cannot, whatever the quad's size,
// because every fragment resolves to the same texel. See `SOLID` for all four
// measurements.
//
// **This is why `solid_box` is not this function.** `solid_box` answers the
// block's rectangle — `(PAD, PAD, SOLID, SOLID)` — and that is exactly the
// measured failing case: a six-by-six source rect is a source rect with extent.
// The two functions differ by the one property that matters and the names say
// which is which.
//
// Every texel of the atlas is white with the coverage in alpha, and
// `sokol_gp`'s stock shader is `texture(tex, uv) * color`, so an opaque white
// texel gives back exactly the tint — including its alpha, so a
// half-transparent tint blends like any other quad.
//
//     (f32 sx, f32 sy, f32 sw, f32 sh) = font.solid_src(&f)
//     draw.texture_rect_region(item, atlas, (x, y, w, h), (sx, sy, sw, sh), colour)
//
// Answers all zeroes for a `Font` with no atlas, which names the atlas's own
// top-left padding pixel — transparent, so a caller that skipped the check
// draws nothing rather than something wrong.
(f32 x, f32 y, f32 w, f32 h) solid_src(^Font f):
    if f == nil || f.pixels == nil || f.atlas_w < 1 || f.atlas_h < 1:
        return (0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0)
    f32 cx = f32(PAD) + f32(SOLID) * 0.5
    f32 cy = f32(PAD) + f32(SOLID) * 0.5
    return (cx, cy, 0.0, 0.0)

// The reserved block's pixel rectangle. A test looks at this; **a drawing
// client wants `solid_src`** and this rectangle is the measured wrong answer
// for it — six texels of extent is 644 wrong pixels of a 64×32 quad.
(i32 x, i32 y, i32 w, i32 h) solid_box(^Font f):
    if f == nil || f.pixels == nil:
        return (0, 0, 0, 0)
    return (PAD, PAD, SOLID, SOLID)

// ── adding a codepoint ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Rasterise `cp` into the first free space on the atlas's retained shelf and
// remember it, so `glyph` and `measure` answer it from then on.
//
// Answers **true when `cp` is available afterwards** — including when it
// already was, so a caller may ask unconditionally per character and pay one
// lookup. False means the codepoint keeps answering `.notdef`, for one of three
// reasons, none of which is an error a caller can repair:
//
//   * this is a fallback face, which has 95 glyphs compiled into it and no
//     outlines to rasterise from;
//   * the face does not have `cp` — `stbtt_FindGlyphIndex` answered 0, which is
//     `.notdef`, and packing the face's own `.notdef` under a second name would
//     be a lie that `present` could not undo;
//   * the atlas has no room left — and this function does not grow it. A caller
//     that would rather have the codepoint than the refusal calls `grow_atlas`
//     and asks again; the package comment has the loop and says when it is
//     safe to run.
//
// On success `atlas_generation` has moved and the drawing surface owes the
// texture an update **before its next frame**.
bool add_codepoint(^Font f, char32 cp):
    if f == nil || f.dense == nil || f.pixels == nil:
        return false
    if glyph(f, cp).present:
        return true
    if f.source != Source.TrueType || f.info == nil:
        return false

    i32 gi = stb_truetype.stbtt_FindGlyphIndex(f.info, i32(cp))
    if gi == 0:
        return false

    Glyph g
    glyph_metrics(f, gi, &g)
    g.present = true
    if g.pw > 0 && g.ph > 0:
        i32 px = 0
        i32 py = 0
        if !shelf_place(f, g.pw, g.ph, &px, &py):
            return false
        g.px = px
        g.py = py

    if !remember(f, cp, g):
        return false

    if g.pw > 0 && g.ph > 0:
        ink_glyph(f, &g, gi)
    f.generation = f.generation + 1
    return true

// Grow the sparse pair by one. The two runs are grown together and
// `extra_used` is only moved once both took, so a failed `#append` cannot leave
// a codepoint pointing at a glyph that was never written.
private bool remember(^Font f, char32 cp, Glyph g):
    if f.extra_cp == nil:
        f.extra_cp = #make([]char32, 0, f.alloc)
        f.extra = #make([]Glyph, 0, f.alloc)
        if f.extra_cp == nil || f.extra == nil:
            return false
    if !#append(f.extra_cp, cp):
        return false
    if !#append(f.extra, g):
        return false
    f.extra_used = f.extra_used + 1
    return true

// How many codepoints have been added past the dense block.
i32 added(^Font f):
    if f == nil:
        return 0
    return f.extra_used

// ── font discovery ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// A monospace face this platform is likely to have, or `""` where there is
// nothing worth guessing.
//
// **A guess, and the signature says so by pairing with `load_file`'s `bool`.**
// This package does not stat the path and does not walk a font directory: a
// path that is wrong costs one failed open and the fallback, which is cheaper
// than a search that is wrong in a different way on every distribution. A
// program that has a real font picker passes its answer to `load_file` and
// never calls this.
//
// Verified 2026-09-06 in the Linux container this was written in:
// `/usr/share/fonts/truetype/dejavu/DejaVuSansMono.ttf` exists.
// **The macOS path is unverified** — there is no Mac here — and it is
// `SFNSMono.ttf` rather than `Menlo.ttc` only because it is the one Apple
// ships as a plain `.ttf`; `load` reads either, because
// `stbtt_GetFontOffsetForIndex` resolves a `ttcf` collection and a bare `.ttf`
// through the same call.
string8 default_path():
    when #OS == #DARWIN:
        return "/System/Library/Fonts/SFNSMono.ttf"
    else when #OS == #WINDOWS:
        return "C:\\Windows\\Fonts\\consola.ttf"
    else when #OS == #LINUX || #OS == #FREEBSD:
        return "/usr/share/fonts/truetype/dejavu/DejaVuSansMono.ttf"
    else:
        return ""

// ── the packer ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// **Shelf (row) packing, in codepoint order, with no sort.**
//
// A cursor walks left to right along the current shelf. When the next box does
// not fit before the right edge, the cursor drops to a new shelf whose top is
// one pixel below the tallest box the previous shelf held. `PAD` pixels sit
// between boxes and around the edge.
//
// The width is chosen **first**: the smallest of 128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048,
// 4096 for which the simulated pack is no taller than it is wide. The height
// is then the next power of two at or above the height actually used, which is
// what leaves the slack a later codepoint packs into.
//
// Sorting by height would waste less — this wastes the difference between each
// box and the tallest on its shelf — but a sort would reorder the glyphs, and
// the ASCII block packed in codepoint order puts the capitals on one shelf and
// the lowercase on the next, which is worth more when reading a dump of the
// atlas by eye than the few percent a sort recovers. Say so rather than
// discovering it.
private bool pack(^Font f):
    i32 width = 128
    i32 used_h = 0
    for:
        used_h = simulate(f, width)
        if used_h > 0 && used_h <= width:
            break
        if width >= 4096:
            if used_h <= 0:
                return false
            break
        width = width * 2

    i32 height = 1
    for:
        if height >= used_h:
            break
        height = height * 2

    // **The slack is deliberate and it is what `add_codepoint` packs into.**
    // Rounding the used height up to a power of two leaves *some* room only by
    // luck — a pack that finishes one row under a power of two leaves one row —
    // so if the rounding did not buy at least one more shelf's worth, take the
    // next power of two. One doubling of an RGBA8 atlas is cheap (a 512-wide,
    // 512-tall atlas costs 1 MB more, where the same doubling cost 256 kB while
    // this package held one byte per pixel) and it is the difference between a
    // font that can
    // learn a codepoint and one that answers `.notdef` forever.
    if height - used_h < shelf_reserve(f):
        height = height * 2

    f.atlas_w = width
    f.atlas_h = height
    f.pixels = #make([]u8, i64(width) * i64(height) * BPP, f.alloc)
    if f.pixels == nil:
        f.atlas_w = 0
        f.atlas_h = 0
        return false
    // White everywhere, alpha zero — **not** the zeroes `#make` already wrote.
    // A transparent black texel fringes dark where a sample straddles a box's
    // edge; the package comment has the whole of that argument.
    i64 zero = 0
    clear_texels(f, zero, i64(width) * i64(height))
    fill_solid(f)

    // The real pass, with the same walk `simulate` just made, writing the
    // placements and leaving the cursor where it stopped. It starts *past* the
    // reserved solid block, which is why the first shelf begins at `SOLID`
    // pixels tall and `PAD + SOLID + PAD` across.
    i32 x = PAD + SOLID + PAD
    i32 y = PAD
    i32 shelf = SOLID
    for idx in 0..<SLOTS:
        ^Glyph g = &f.dense[i64(idx)]
        if g.pw <= 0 || g.ph <= 0:
            g.px = 0
            g.py = 0
            continue
        if x + g.pw + PAD > width:
            x = PAD
            y = y + shelf + PAD
            shelf = 0
        g.px = x
        g.py = y
        x = x + g.pw + PAD
        if g.ph > shelf:
            shelf = g.ph

    f.shelf_x = x
    f.shelf_y = y
    f.shelf_h = shelf
    f.generation = 1
    return true

// The height the shelf walk would use at `width`, or 0 when a single box is
// wider than the whole atlas — which is what makes the caller's ladder step up
// rather than pack an unplaceable glyph at x = PAD and let it run off the
// right edge.
private i32 simulate(^Font f, i32 width):
    i32 x = PAD + SOLID + PAD
    i32 y = PAD
    i32 shelf = SOLID
    for idx in 0..<SLOTS:
        ^Glyph g = &f.dense[i64(idx)]
        if g.pw <= 0 || g.ph <= 0:
            continue
        if g.pw + 2 * PAD > width:
            return 0
        if x + g.pw + PAD > width:
            x = PAD
            y = y + shelf + PAD
            shelf = 0
        x = x + g.pw + PAD
        if g.ph > shelf:
            shelf = g.ph
    return y + shelf + PAD

// How much free height below the packed block is worth having: **four shelves
// of this face**, and the number is four because it was measured.
//
// The power-of-two rounding alone leaves whatever it happens to leave, and what
// it happens to leave swings wildly with the size. Measured on DejaVu Sans Mono
// with the rounding alone, counting how many further codepoints
// `add_codepoint` took before the shelf ran out: **12 px → 110, 16 px → 46,
// 18 px → 126, 24 px → 171, 32 px → 95.** Forty-six at 16 px — the size a UI
// actually runs at — is barely Latin-1, and nothing about the size predicts it.
// One more doubling costs one more atlas, which at that size is 128 kB of
// RGBA8 — four times the 32 kB it cost as alpha-8, and still the cheapest thing
// in sight next to a face that answers `.notdef` forever.
// With this reserve the same five are **110, 180, 126, 171, 241**: the two that
// were thin doubled, the three that already had room did not, and the worst
// case stopped being a coin toss.
private i32 shelf_reserve(^Font f):
    i32 want = 4 * (i32(f.pixel_height) + 2 * PAD)
    if want < 8:
        return 8
    return want

// Write the reserved fully-opaque block. Called once, right after the atlas is
// cleared and before any glyph is inked, so nothing can be packed over it.
private void fill_solid(^Font f):
    for y in 0..<SOLID:
        i64 row = i64(PAD + y) * i64(f.atlas_w)
        for x in 0..<SOLID:
            i64 at = (row + i64(PAD + x)) * BPP
            f.pixels[at] = 255
            f.pixels[at + 1] = 255
            f.pixels[at + 2] = 255
            f.pixels[at + 3] = 255

// Place one box on the retained shelf, or answer false when the atlas has no
// room left. This is the same walk `pack` makes, one box at a time, and it is
// what makes the cursor worth retaining.
private bool shelf_place(^Font f, i32 w, i32 h, ^i32 out_x, ^i32 out_y):
    i32 x = f.shelf_x
    i32 y = f.shelf_y
    i32 shelf = f.shelf_h
    if w + 2 * PAD > f.atlas_w:
        return false
    if x + w + PAD > f.atlas_w:
        x = PAD
        y = y + shelf + PAD
        shelf = 0
    if y + h + PAD > f.atlas_h:
        return false
    out_x^ = x
    out_y^ = y
    f.shelf_x = x + w + PAD
    f.shelf_y = y
    if h > shelf:
        shelf = h
    f.shelf_h = shelf
    return true

// ── the image, and the scratch stb makes it necessary ─────────────────────

// Fill texels `from` up to `upto` — **texel indices, not byte offsets** — with
// the untouched texel: white, alpha zero.
//
// **Not four zeroes**, and this is the one place in the package where that
// mistake would be invisible. Four zeroes is a transparent *black*: a bilinear
// sample that straddles a glyph's edge interpolates towards the colour beside
// it, so black-transparent padding darkens every border in the atlas and
// white-transparent padding cannot. The difference is only visible on a screen,
// which is the worst place to find it, so the fill is written once here and
// every caller goes through it.
private void clear_texels(^Font f, i64 from, i64 upto):
    for i in from..<upto:
        i64 at = i * BPP
        f.pixels[at] = 255
        f.pixels[at + 1] = 255
        f.pixels[at + 2] = 255
        f.pixels[at + 3] = 0

// The scratch this face rasterises through, sized to the largest coverage box
// it can produce.
//
// `stbtt_GetFontBoundingBox` is the union of every outline in the face, in
// design units, so scaling it bounds every box `stbtt_GetGlyphBitmapBox` can
// answer. Two pixels of slack on each axis cover stb's own rounding, which
// floors the low edge of a box and ceils the high one and so can answer a box a
// pixel larger than the scaled extent in each direction.
//
// A floor of one cell squared, because a face whose `head` table understates
// its own outlines would otherwise get a scratch too small for its first glyph
// — and `fit_scratch` is the second guard for exactly that.
private ref []u8 make_scratch(^Font f):
    i32 x0 = 0
    i32 y0 = 0
    i32 x1 = 0
    i32 y1 = 0
    stb_truetype.stbtt_GetFontBoundingBox(f.info, &x0, &y0, &x1, &y1)
    i64 w = i64(f32(x1 - x0) * f.scale) + 2
    i64 h = i64(f32(y1 - y0) * f.scale) + 2
    i64 floor = i64(f.pixel_height) + 2
    if w < floor:
        w = floor
    if h < floor:
        h = floor
    return #make([]u8, w * h, f.alloc)

// Make sure the scratch holds a `w` by `h` coverage box, growing it where the
// face's own bounding box did not bound it.
//
// A font file that understates its bounding box is a file, not a hypothesis,
// and a rasteriser writing past its buffer because one lied is not a failure
// mode worth having. This costs one comparison per glyph and answers `false`
// only when the allocator refuses, in which case the glyph is left blank rather
// than written somewhere it does not belong.
//
// **The `#resize` has never fired here.** Making it answer `false` instead —
// so that a glyph too large for the initial sizing draws nothing — leaves the
// whole suite green, over the dense ASCII block at two sizes, `.notdef`, and
// the two-hundred-odd codepoints `add_codepoint` takes on DejaVu Sans Mono
// before the shelf runs out. So this is a guard against a file, not a path the
// packing relies on.
private bool fit_scratch(^Font f, i32 w, i32 h):
    if f.scratch == nil:
        return false
    i64 want = i64(w) * i64(h)
    if #len(f.scratch) >= want:
        return true
    return #resize(f.scratch, want)

// Clear the `w` by `h` box at the front of the scratch.
//
// stb's rasteriser writes a whole scanline for every row of the box it is
// given, so in principle nothing stale survives — but the scratch is reused by
// every glyph of the face, so a byte it did not write is the *previous* glyph's
// ink showing through this one, and "in principle" is a claim about an internal
// of a vendored header that this package cannot test. `w * h` stores against a
// rasterisation that costs far more than that is the cheaper side of the trade.
private void zero_scratch(^Font f, i32 w, i32 h):
    i64 n = i64(w) * i64(h)
    i64 zero = 0
    for i in zero..<n:
        f.scratch[i] = 0

// Widen a `w` by `h` coverage box from the front of the scratch into the atlas
// at `(px, py)`: white, with the coverage byte in alpha.
private void expand(^Font f, i32 px, i32 py, i32 w, i32 h):
    for y in 0..<h:
        i64 src = i64(y) * i64(w)
        i64 dst = (i64(py + y) * i64(f.atlas_w) + i64(px)) * BPP
        for x in 0..<w:
            i64 at = dst + i64(x) * BPP
            f.pixels[at] = 255
            f.pixels[at + 1] = 255
            f.pixels[at + 2] = 255
            f.pixels[at + 3] = f.scratch[src + i64(x)]

// Rasterise a codepoint through the scratch and into `g`'s packed box.
private void ink_codepoint(^Font f, ^Glyph g, i32 cp):
    if !fit_scratch(f, g.pw, g.ph):
        return
    zero_scratch(f, g.pw, g.ph)
    stb_truetype.stbtt_MakeCodepointBitmap(f.info, &f.scratch[0], g.pw, g.ph, g.pw,
                                           f.scale, f.scale, cp)
    expand(f, g.px, g.py, g.pw, g.ph)

// The same for a glyph index, which is how `add_codepoint` and the face's own
// `.notdef` reach the rasteriser. The stride handed to stb is `g.pw` — the
// scratch's own width — where it used to be `atlas_w`, and that substitution is
// the whole of what the scratch changes at these two call sites.
private void ink_glyph(^Font f, ^Glyph g, i32 gi):
    if !fit_scratch(f, g.pw, g.ph):
        return
    zero_scratch(f, g.pw, g.ph)
    stb_truetype.stbtt_MakeGlyphBitmap(f.info, &f.scratch[0], g.pw, g.ph, g.pw,
                                       f.scale, f.scale, gi)
    expand(f, g.px, g.py, g.pw, g.ph)

// ── glyph metrics, `.notdef`, and the heights a text view wants ───────────

// Fill `g`'s box, bearings and advance from glyph index `gi` of a TrueType
// face. The one place `stbtt`'s glyph-index half is called; every other call in
// this package goes through a codepoint.
private void glyph_metrics(^Font f, i32 gi, ^Glyph g):
    i32 x0 = 0
    i32 y0 = 0
    i32 x1 = 0
    i32 y1 = 0
    stb_truetype.stbtt_GetGlyphBitmapBox(f.info, gi, f.scale, f.scale, &x0, &y0, &x1, &y1)
    i32 adv = 0
    i32 lsb = 0
    stb_truetype.stbtt_GetGlyphHMetrics(f.info, gi, &adv, &lsb)
    g.pw = x1 - x0
    g.ph = y1 - y0
    if g.pw < 0: g.pw = 0
    if g.ph < 0: g.ph = 0
    g.bearing_x = f32(x0)
    g.bearing_y = f32(y0)
    g.advance = f32(adv) * f.scale

// The nominal width of one character cell. `'0'` rather than `'M'` or the
// widest advance in the face, because a digit is the character a monospace face
// is designed around and a proportional face's digits are near its average; a
// maximum would make a tab stop absurd on any face with a wide `W`.
private f32 nominal_cell(^Font f):
    f32 z = f.dense[i64('0') - i64(FIRST)].advance
    if z > 0.0:
        return z
    f32 sp = f.dense[i64(' ') - i64(FIRST)].advance
    if sp > 0.0:
        return sp
    return f.pixel_height * 0.5

// The box a drawn `.notdef` occupies: a rectangle a bit narrower than the cell,
// standing on the baseline and rising to about cap height, which is what every
// face's own `.notdef` looks like.
private void synth_notdef(^Font f, ^Glyph g):
    i32 w = i32(f.cell_px) - 2
    if w < 3:
        w = 3
    i32 h = i32(f.ascent_px * 0.72)
    if h < 4:
        h = 4
    g.pw = w
    g.ph = h
    g.bearing_x = 1.0
    g.bearing_y = 0.0 - f32(h)

// Ink `.notdef` into its packed box. A TrueType face draws its own glyph 0;
// anything else — a face whose glyph 0 is empty, and the built-in bitmap, which
// has no glyph 0 at all — gets a hollow rectangle whose border is `s` pixels,
// where `s` is the bitmap's integer scale and 1 for a real face.
private void ink_notdef(^Font f, ^Glyph g, i32 s):
    if g.pw <= 0 || g.ph <= 0:
        return
    if f.info != nil && stb_truetype.stbtt_IsGlyphEmpty(f.info, 0) == 0:
        ink_glyph(f, g, 0)
        return
    i32 t = s
    if t < 1:
        t = 1
    if t * 2 >= g.pw || t * 2 >= g.ph:
        t = 1
    // Only the alpha byte is written: the three colour bytes are already 255,
    // put there by `clear_texels` before anything was packed, and every texel of
    // this atlas keeps them.
    for y in 0..<g.ph:
        i64 row = i64(g.py + y) * i64(f.atlas_w)
        bool edge_row = y < t || y >= g.ph - t
        for x in 0..<g.pw:
            if edge_row || x < t || x >= g.pw - t:
                f.pixels[(row + i64(g.px + x)) * BPP + 3] = 255

// Cap height and x-height, **measured off this atlas's own ink** rather than
// read out of `OS/2`.
//
// The face's `sCapHeight` and `sxHeight` exist only from `OS/2` version 2, so
// half the faces on a machine do not carry them and `stbtt` exposes no way to
// reach the table anyway (`stbtt__find_table` is `static`, and
// `stbtt_GetFontVMetricsOS2` answers the typographic *vertical* trio and
// nothing else). The top of the rasterised `H` box **is** the cap height in
// pixels and the top of the `x` box **is** the x-height, exactly, for this
// rasterisation — which is the number a caller wants, since it is placing a
// rule against ink it can see. It also answers for the built-in bitmap, which
// has no tables at all.
private void measure_heights(^Font f):
    f.cap_px = 0.0
    f.xh_px = 0.0
    Glyph h = f.dense[i64('H') - i64(FIRST)]
    if h.ph > 0:
        f.cap_px = 0.0 - h.bearing_y
    Glyph x = f.dense[i64('x') - i64(FIRST)]
    if x.ph > 0:
        f.xh_px = 0.0 - x.bearing_y

// The four bytes of `post` that say where an underline goes, read straight out
// of the font file this package already keeps a copy of.
//
// **`stbtt` cannot answer this and there is no binding to add.**
// `stbtt__find_table` is `static` in the header, so the table directory is not
// reachable from outside — but the directory is eleven lines of big-endian
// arithmetic and `Font` already owns the bytes and the offset this face starts
// at, so it is read here rather than gone without.
//
// A face with no `post` table, and the built-in bitmap, get a derived pair and
// `ul_real` says so: one third of the way to the descender, one fourteenth of
// the pixel height thick and never thinner than a pixel. That is a guess and it
// is labelled as one.
private void read_underline(^Font f):
    f.ul_real = false
    f.ul_pos_px = (0.0 - f.descent_px) * 0.33
    f.ul_thick_px = f.pixel_height / 14.0
    if f.ul_thick_px < 1.0:
        f.ul_thick_px = 1.0
    if f.ttf == nil:
        return
    (i64 at, i64 n, bool found) = find_table(f, "post")
    if !found || n < 12:
        return
    // `underlinePosition` is an FWORD in the face's y-up design units, so a
    // value below the baseline is negative there and positive here.
    f32 pos = f32(be_i16(f, at + 8)) * f.scale
    f32 thick = f32(be_i16(f, at + 10)) * f.scale
    if thick <= 0.0:
        return
    f.ul_pos_px = 0.0 - pos
    f.ul_thick_px = thick
    f.ul_real = true

// Find a table in this face's sfnt directory. `tag` is its four-character name.
private (i64 at, i64 nbytes, bool found) find_table(^Font f, string8 tag):
    i64 start = i64(f.ttf_at)
    i64 total = #len(f.ttf)
    if start < 0 || start + 12 > total || #len(tag) != 4:
        return (0, 0, false)
    i64 ntables = i64(be_u16(f, start + 4))
    i64 dir = start + 12
    if dir + ntables * 16 > total:
        return (0, 0, false)
    i64 zero = 0
    for i in zero..<ntables:
        i64 rec = dir + i * 16
        bool same = true
        for k in zero..<i64(4):
            if f.ttf[rec + k] != u8(tag[k]):
                same = false
        if !same:
            continue
        i64 at = i64(be_u32(f, rec + 8))
        i64 n = i64(be_u32(f, rec + 12))
        if at < 0 || n < 0 || at + n > total:
            return (0, 0, false)
        return (at, n, true)
    return (0, 0, false)

private u32 be_u16(^Font f, i64 at):
    return (u32(f.ttf[at]) << 8) | u32(f.ttf[at + 1])

private i32 be_i16(^Font f, i64 at):
    i32 v = i32(be_u16(f, at))
    if v >= 0x8000:
        return v - 0x10000
    return v

private u32 be_u32(^Font f, i64 at):
    return (be_u16(f, at) << 16) | be_u16(f, at + 2)

// ── the built-in bitmap ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The tightest box of set bits in one 8x8 glyph, and whether there were any.
// Columns and rows are inclusive.
private (i32 c0, i32 r0, i32 c1, i32 r1, bool inked) ink_box(u64 bits):
    i32 c0 = 8
    i32 r0 = 8
    i32 c1 = -1
    i32 r1 = -1
    for r in 0..<8:
        u64 row = (bits >> u64(8 * r)) & 0xFF
        if row == 0:
            continue
        if r < r0: r0 = r
        if r > r1: r1 = r
        for c in 0..<8:
            if (row >> u64(c)) & 1 != 0:
                if c < c0: c0 = c
                if c > c1: c1 = c
    if c1 < 0:
        return (0, 0, 0, 0, false)
    return (c0, r0, c1, r1, true)

// Draw one 8x8 glyph into the atlas at `g`'s placement, scaled `s` times in
// both directions. Coverage is 0 or 255: this is a bitmap font and an
// intermediate value would be a lie about an edge nothing measured.
//
// **No scratch, and no stb.** The fallback face's source is a bit per pixel in
// a `u64`, so this writes the alpha byte of each set bit's texel straight into
// the atlas; the three colour bytes are already 255 and stay so. The scratch
// exists for stb's byte-stride rasteriser and nothing else needs it.
private void blit(^Font f, ^Glyph g, u64 bits, i32 c0, i32 r0, i32 s):
    for yy in 0..<g.ph:
        i32 srow = r0 + yy / s
        u64 row = (bits >> u64(8 * srow)) & 0xFF
        i64 base = i64(g.py + yy) * i64(f.atlas_w) + i64(g.px)
        for xx in 0..<g.pw:
            i32 scol = c0 + xx / s
            if (row >> u64(scol)) & 1 != 0:
                f.pixels[(base + i64(xx)) * BPP + 3] = 255

// ── odds ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

private bool all_advances_equal(^Font f):
    if f.dense == nil:
        return false
    f32 first = f.dense[0].advance
    for i in 1..<DENSE:
        f32 a = f.dense[i64(i)].advance
        f32 d = a - first
        if d < 0.0:
            d = -d
        if d > 0.01:
            return false
    return true

// Read a whole file. `nil` and `false` when it cannot be opened or is empty.
//
// The two windows are the `char8`/`u8` boundary and nothing more interesting:
// `io.read_bytes` fills a `[]char8` and `stb_truetype` wants a `^u8`, both
// `uint8_t` in C and two shapes in Dado, so the buffer is allocated once as
// `[]u8` and handed to `io` through a `Chars` window over the same address.
private type Chars: ^char8

private (ref []u8 bytes, bool ok) read_all(cstring path):
    ref []u8 nothing
    i64 n = fs.bytes(path)
    if n <= 0:
        return (nothing, false)

    ^io.FILE stream = io.open_read(path)
    if !io.opened(stream):
        return (nothing, false)

    ref []u8 buf = #make([]u8, n)
    if buf == nil:
        _ = io.close(stream)
        return (nothing, false)

    Chars c = Chars(rawptr(&buf[0]))
    i64 got = io.read_bytes(stream, c[0..<n])
    _ = io.close(stream)
    if got != n:
        #delete(buf)
        return (nothing, false)
    return (buf, true)

// ── the fallback face ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// **`font8x8_basic` by Daniel Hepper — Public Domain.**
//
// From <https://github.com/dhepper/font8x8>, itself derived from Marcel
// Sondaar's `font8x8.h` and the public-domain IBM PC BIOS VGA fonts. The
// upstream header states *"License: Public Domain"* on both its own work and
// what it is based on, so there is nothing to attach a licence file to and
// nothing this package has to ask of a program that links it. The provenance
// is written here because a font with no provenance is the one thing in a
// repository nobody can check later.
//
// **One `u64` per glyph, not eight bytes**, because 8 rows x 8 columns is
// exactly 64 bits and a 95-element table of integers is a literal a human can
// read past; 760 separate bytes is not. Row `r` is `(bits >> 8*r) & 0xFF`, and
// within a row **bit 0 is the leftmost column** — the upstream file's bit
// order, kept rather than reversed, so a byte here can be compared against the
// upstream table without a transform in the way.
//
// Codepoints 32..126 inclusive, in order. The upstream table covers 0..127;
// the control codes and DEL are dropped because there is no glyph to draw for
// them and a blank in the atlas is indistinguishable from a missing one.
const [95]u64 FALLBACK_BITS = [
    0x0000000000000000, 0x00180018183C3C18, 0x0000000000003636, 0x0036367F367F3636,   // ' !"#'
    0x000C1F301E033E0C, 0x0063660C18336300, 0x006E333B6E1C361C, 0x0000000000030606,   // "$%&'"
    0x00180C0606060C18, 0x00060C1818180C06, 0x0000663CFF3C6600, 0x00000C0C3F0C0C00,   // '()*+'
    0x060C0C0000000000, 0x000000003F000000, 0x000C0C0000000000, 0x000103060C183060,   // ',-./'
    0x003E676F7B73633E, 0x003F0C0C0C0C0E0C, 0x003F33061C30331E, 0x001E33301C30331E,   // '0123'
    0x0078307F33363C38, 0x001E3330301F033F, 0x001E33331F03061C, 0x000C0C0C1830333F,   // '4567'
    0x001E33331E33331E, 0x000E18303E33331E, 0x000C0C00000C0C00, 0x060C0C00000C0C00,   // '89:;'
    0x00180C0603060C18, 0x00003F00003F0000, 0x00060C1830180C06, 0x000C000C1830331E,   // '<=>?'
    0x001E037B7B7B633E, 0x0033333F33331E0C, 0x003F66663E66663F, 0x003C66030303663C,   // '@ABC'
    0x001F36666666361F, 0x007F46161E16467F, 0x000F06161E16467F, 0x007C66730303663C,   // 'DEFG'
    0x003333333F333333, 0x001E0C0C0C0C0C1E, 0x001E333330303078, 0x006766361E366667,   // 'HIJK'
    0x007F66460606060F, 0x0063636B7F7F7763, 0x006363737B6F6763, 0x001C36636363361C,   // 'LMNO'
    0x000F06063E66663F, 0x00381E3B3333331E, 0x006766363E66663F, 0x001E33380E07331E,   // 'PQRS'
    0x001E0C0C0C0C2D3F, 0x003F333333333333, 0x000C1E3333333333, 0x0063777F6B636363,   // 'TUVW'
    0x0063361C1C366363, 0x001E0C0C1E333333, 0x007F664C1831637F, 0x001E06060606061E,   // 'XYZ['
    0x00406030180C0603, 0x001E18181818181E, 0x0000000063361C08, 0xFF00000000000000,   // '\\]^_'
    0x0000000000180C0C, 0x006E333E301E0000, 0x003B66663E060607, 0x001E3303331E0000,   // '`abc'
    0x006E33333E303038, 0x001E033F331E0000, 0x000F06060F06361C, 0x1F303E33336E0000,   // 'defg'
    0x006766666E360607, 0x001E0C0C0C0E000C, 0x1E33333030300030, 0x0067361E36660607,   // 'hijk'
    0x001E0C0C0C0C0C0E, 0x00636B7F7F330000, 0x00333333331F0000, 0x001E3333331E0000,   // 'lmno'
    0x0F063E66663B0000, 0x78303E33336E0000, 0x000F06666E3B0000, 0x001F301E033E0000,   // 'pqrs'
    0x00182C0C0C3E0C08, 0x006E333333330000, 0x000C1E3333330000, 0x00367F7F6B630000,   // 'tuvw'
    0x0063361C36630000, 0x1F303E3333330000, 0x003F260C193F0000, 0x00380C0C070C0C38,   // 'xyz{'
    0x0018181800181818, 0x00070C0C380C0C07, 0x0000000000003B6E   // '|}~'
]
